    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm looking into some premature longitudinal cracking on a new asphalt section we laid in Phoenix recently. Can you give me a summary of the common causes for this kind of pavement failure, specifically looking at thermal expansion and bitumen issues?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Premature longitudinal cracking in asphalt, particularly in hot climates like Phoenix, often stems from excessive thermal expansion that exceeds the material's ability to accommodate stress. If the bitumen lacks adequate flexibility at low temperatures (a problem known as cold-temperature failure), it becomes brittle and cannot bridge the gap between contracting pavement sections, leading to cracks. Additionally, improper mix design or poor compaction during laydown can prevent the pavement from properly absorbing these thermal stresses, accelerating the formation of longitudinal fissures.",
      "source": "target",
